---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'connect', label: 'Connect the server' },
   { id: 'run', label: 'Ask for an axe run' },
   { id: 'driver', label: 'Ask for a session' },
   { id: 'prompts', label: 'Prompts that work' },
   { id: 'pitfalls', label: 'Two failure modes' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test from an AI agent"
   summary="The MCP server gives a coding agent 13 tools: WCAG lookup, search, and axe rule mapping, axe runs, the accessibility tree, the full audit loop, screen reader session control and actions, and host diagnostics. It runs locally over stdio and opens no network port."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="connect">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 1</p>
            <h2>Connect the server</h2>
         </div>
         <p>Add a11ied to the MCP configuration your editor or agent reads, then restart it.</p>
         <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "a11ied": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "a11ied", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
         <p>
            Ask the agent to call <code>doctor</code> as its first action. The result lists the
            browser a11ied will use and the status of each screen reader target, which tells the
            agent what it can actually run.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Resources too</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            The server also exposes seven read-only resources, including the criteria index and the
            coverage table for WCAG 2.1 and 2.2. See the
            <a href="/reference/mcp">MCP reference</a>.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="run">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 2</p>
         <h2>Ask for an axe run</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Start your development server, then give the agent the exact route and the level you want
         tested.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Open http://localhost:3000/account/settings.
Use a11ied to run axe at WCAG level AA.
For each violation, give me the selector, the impact, and the failing HTML.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The agent calls <code>run_axe</code> with <code>target</code> and
         <code>level: "AA"</code>. Omitting <code>criterion</code>, <code>level</code>, and
         <code>ruleIds</code> runs every mapped rule. The response carries a <code>verdict</code>
         object and <code>exitCode</code> 4 when a violation was found.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="driver">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 3</p>
         <h2>Ask for a screen reader session</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Use a session for labels, focus order, dialogs, menus, and validation messages. An axe run
         reports none of those in sequence.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Use a11ied to walk the checkout form with the screen reader.
Move through every field in order and collect the spoken phrase log.
Tell me which controls announce without a name and where the order
does not match the visual order.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The agent calls <code>sr_session</code> with <code>action: "start"</code>, then
         <code>sr_action</code> for each step, then <code>sr_expect</code> to check what was
         announced. For VoiceOver or NVDA, open the page in a browser before the agent starts. For
         the virtual reader, the agent passes <code>url</code> and <code>allowVirtual: true</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         Remind the agent to call <code>sr_session</code> with <code>action: "stop"</code> when
         it finishes. A session left open holds the screen reader under automation.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="prompts">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Prompts</p>
         <h2>Jobs worth handing over</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Review a branch</strong>
            <span>Run axe on the routes the diff touches and compare each violation against the intended behavior.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Test a dialog</strong>
            <span>Open it, move focus through it, close it, and report where focus landed at each step.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Work one criterion</strong>
            <span>Look up the criterion, read its coverage, inspect the page, then run the test the strategy names.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>Narrow a bug report</strong>
            <span>Reproduce the reported step, capture the phrase log around it, and quote the exact announcement.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="pitfalls">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Watch for</p>
         <h2>Two ways an agent gets this wrong</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The first is reporting a clean axe run as conformance. axe rules decide 8 of the 24 level AA
         criteria. Ask the agent for the coverage state next to every result.
      </p>
      <p>
         The second is quoting virtual reader output as VoiceOver behavior. The session response
         carries <code>targetType</code>, either <code>real</code> or <code>simulated</code>. Ask the
         agent to include it. The <a href="/guides/agent-skill">agent skill</a> builds both rules
         into the model's instructions.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
